Codex now reads the same SKILL.md folders as every other agent that follows the open Agent Skills format, so the open question is which of the thousands of public skills are worth the context budget. The agentskills.codes catalog indexes 11,524 skills as of November 26, 2026, and a plain search for "codex" returns 191 of them. Most of those 191 are Claude Code skills that mention Codex in passing. Very few were written for how Codex actually discovers and loads a skill.

We picked 14 skills that fit web development and day-to-day coding in Codex, read each SKILL.md from its GitHub source, checked what the folder really contains, and ranked them. **Webapp Testing** is the best first install because it ships a working server-lifecycle script and has the highest install count of the testing skills. **Frontend Design** wins for UI quality, **GH Fix CI** for pull-request work, and **UI UX Pro Max** has by far the most catalog installs (1,909). One finding changes how you should install most of them: the catalog's hosted zip contains only `SKILL.md`, so skills that depend on scripts need a GitHub install instead. Details are in the install section.

{% youtube id="4IWTdkwa9Js" title="Codex Skills Tutorial (2026): Build Your First Skill and Stop Repeating Prompts" /%}

*A third-party walkthrough by James NoCode on writing a first Codex skill. We did not review its claims line by line; OpenAI's own documentation is the authority for everything below.*

## Short answer: which Codex skills to install first

- **Best overall:** [Webapp Testing](https://agentskills.codes/skills/webapp-testing). 353 catalog installs, bundled `with_server.py` to start your dev server, and a clear "reconnaissance then action" procedure for browser checks.
- **Best for front-end design quality:** [Frontend Design](https://agentskills.codes/skills/frontend-design). 481 installs and a rewrite dated September 3, 2026 that targets generic AI design defaults.
- **Best for pull requests and CI:** [GH Fix CI](https://agentskills.codes/skills/gh-fix-ci) (diagnose failing GitHub Actions checks, then plan, then fix only after approval) and [GH Address Comments](https://agentskills.codes/skills/gh-address-comments) (numbered review threads, you choose which to fix).
- **Best for security:** [Security Best Practices](https://agentskills.codes/skills/security-best-practices). Ten reference files, including a Next.js server file, a React front-end file and an Express file.
- **Best free option:** all 14 are free to install. The only cost is your Codex plan usage, covered in the pricing section.
- **Best open source:** every pick lives in a public GitHub repository. Eight of the 14 upstream repos report an MIT or Apache-2.0 license through the GitHub API. The other six come from `openai/skills` and `anthropics/skills`, which report no license to the API, though each of those skill folders ships its own LICENSE.txt.
- **Best for browser automation outside tests:** [Agent Browser](https://agentskills.codes/skills/agent-browser) (54,123 GitHub stars) if you can install its CLI, [Playwright Skill](https://agentskills.codes/skills/playwright-browser-automation) if you prefer plain Node scripts.
- **Biggest trap:** [Next.js Developer](https://agentskills.codes/skills/nextjs-developer) has 328 installs, but the catalog copy is a single descriptive paragraph with no instructions. We dropped it. More on that under "What we left out".

## Where Codex loads skills from

This is the part most "best skills" lists skip, and it decides whether your install does anything. We read OpenAI's skills page (the page is served from developers.openai.com and learn.chatgpt.com) on November 26, 2026 and cross-checked it against the `openai/codex` source on GitHub.

**What the docs say.** Codex reads skills from repository, user, admin and system locations. For a repository, it scans `.agents/skills` in every directory from your current working directory up to the repository root.

| Scope | Location | Use it for |
| :-- | :-- | :-- |
| Repo | `$CWD/.agents/skills` | Skills for one module or microservice |
| Repo | `$CWD/../.agents/skills` | Skills for a shared parent folder |
| Repo | `$REPO_ROOT/.agents/skills` | Skills every contributor to the repo should get |
| User | `$HOME/.agents/skills` | Your personal skills across all repos |
| Admin | `/etc/codex/skills` | Machine or container defaults (SDK scripts, automation) |
| System | Bundled with Codex | `skill-creator` and `skill-installer`, enabled by default |

Rules worth knowing, all from the same page:

- Codex follows symlinked skill folders.
- If two skills share a `name`, Codex does not merge them. Both can appear in selectors.
- Codex detects skill changes automatically; if an update does not appear, restart Codex.
- Skills use progressive disclosure: Codex starts with each skill's name, description and file path, then reads the full SKILL.md only when it picks the skill. That initial list is capped at 2% of the model's context window (8,000 characters if the window is unknown). When many skills are installed, Codex shortens descriptions first and may omit some with a warning. You can raise the budget with `skills.max_context_tokens`, up to 10,000 tokens.
- You can invoke a skill explicitly with `$skill-name` or the `/skills` command in the CLI or IDE extension, or let Codex match it implicitly from the description.
- To disable a skill without deleting it, add a `[[skills.config]]` entry with `enabled = false` to `~/.codex/config.toml`. To turn off the bundled system skills for one project, set `[skills.bundled] enabled = false` in that project's trusted `.codex/config.toml`.

**What the installer does.** The bundled `skill-installer` script (`install-skill-from-github.py` in `openai/codex`) writes to `$CODEX_HOME/skills`, which defaults to `~/.codex/skills`. That path is not in the docs table above. We looked for it in the source: `codex-rs/exec-server/src/discoverV2/capability_locations.rs` builds a skill root named `codex-home-skills` from `codex_home.join("skills")`, so the installer's target is a real discovery root in the current source, even though the docs list only `.agents/skills`, `/etc/codex/skills` and the system bundle. We did not launch Codex and watch it pick up a skill from either path.

**Our recommendation.** Put repo skills in `.agents/skills` and personal skills in `~/.agents/skills`, because those are the paths the docs promise. That also helps portability: Cursor, Gemini CLI, OpenCode and Hermes Agent all document `.agents/skills` as a shared location (see the works-with matrix below), so one folder serves several tools.

{% callout type="info" %}
Distribution is the other half of the story. OpenAI's docs say direct skill folders are for local authoring and repo-scoped workflows, and recommend packaging skills as plugins when you want to share them. OpenAI announced Agent Plugins on August 6, 2026 as an open standard that packages Agent Skills with MCP server configuration. This article ranks skills, not plugins, but if your team wants one install for several skills, look at plugins.
{% /callout %}

{% figure src="https://learn.chatgpt.com/og/docs/build-skills.png" alt="Social card image for OpenAI's Build skills documentation page" caption="OpenAI's Build skills docs page, the source for the Codex skill locations above. Source: OpenAI" /%}

