| [Next.js Best Practices](https://agentskills.codes/skills/nextjs-best-practices) | 651 | `curl -L https://agentskills.codes/api/skills/download/651 -o skill.zip && unzip skill.zip -d ~/.agents/skills/nextjs-best-practices` | No |
| [Code Reviewer](https://agentskills.codes/skills/code-reviewer) | 527 | `curl -L https://agentskills.codes/api/skills/download/527 -o skill.zip && unzip skill.zip -d ~/.agents/skills/code-reviewer` | No |

We downloaded the zip for each id in this table (including Playwright Skill, id 58) on November 26, 2026 and each returned HTTP 200 and held one `SKILL.md`. We unpacked several of them in a scratch folder to compare with GitHub; for nine of them (Frontend Design, Webapp Testing, Skill Installer, GH Fix CI, GH Address Comments, Security Best Practices, Figma Implement Design, Web Design Guidelines, Next.js Best Practices) the instruction body matched the upstream file after we stripped front matter and normalized whitespace. We did not do that comparison for the other five.

### Works-with matrix

All six clients below load the same SKILL.md folder format. "Documented" means the client's own docs list the path; we did not install and run each skill in each client.

| Client | Skills location the docs list | Reads `.agents/skills`? |
| :-- | :-- | :-- |
| Codex | `.agents/skills` (repo), `~/.agents/skills` (user), `/etc/codex/skills` (admin) | Yes, documented |
| Claude Code | `~/.claude/skills`, project `.claude/skills` | Not in the page we read |
| Cursor | `.agents/skills/`, `.cursor/skills/`, `~/.agents/skills/`, `~/.cursor/skills/` (its page also lists `.claude/skills/` and `.codex/skills/`) | Yes, documented |
| Gemini CLI | `~/.gemini/skills/` or the `~/.agents/skills/` alias, and `.gemini/skills/` or `.agents/skills/` in a workspace | Yes, documented |
| OpenCode | `~/.config/opencode/skills`, `.opencode/skills`, plus compatibility paths `~/.claude/skills`, `~/.agents/skills`, `.claude/skills`, `.agents/skills` | Yes, documented |
| Hermes Agent | `~/.hermes/skills/`; shared directories such as `~/.agents/skills` can be added through `skills.external_dirs` | Yes, via `external_dirs` |

The practical consequence: one copy in `~/.agents/skills` covers Codex, Cursor, Gemini CLI and OpenCode, and Hermes with one config line. Claude Code needs its own folder or a symlink. Per-skill caveats still apply: GH Fix CI needs `gh`, Agent Browser needs its CLI, and UI UX Pro Max's catalog copy points at a Claude plugin path.

### Related catalog entries and MCP servers

- [OpenAI Docs](https://agentskills.codes/skills/openai-docs) (OpenAI, 3 installs). Official documentation lookup through the developers.openai.com docs MCP server; its SKILL.md has a section on Codex self-knowledge, useful when you want Codex to answer questions about its own configuration.
- [Vercel Deploy](https://agentskills.codes/skills/vercel-deploy) (OpenAI-curated, 4 installs). Deployment and preview management; pairs with the Next.js picks. We did not read it in depth.
- [Yeet](https://agentskills.codes/skills/yeet) (OpenAI-curated, 2 installs). A one-shot Git-to-pull-request flow, per its catalog description; a natural precursor to GH Fix CI.
- [TypeScript Review](https://agentskills.codes/skills/typescript-review) (Metabase, 39 installs). A model for writing your own repo-specific review skill: review dimensions, priority order, and blind spots like accessibility and bundle size.
- [React Best Practices](https://agentskills.codes/skills/react-best-practices) (Redpanda, 22 installs). 29 performance rules per its catalog description; specific to that project's stack.
- [Playwright MCP](https://agentskills.codes/skills/playwright-mcp) (33 installs). For when you would rather connect a browser through an MCP server than run scripts.
- The [Figma MCP server](https://developers.figma.com/docs/figma-mcp-server/) is a prerequisite for Figma Implement Design; its docs page returned 200 on November 26, 2026.

If you also use Claude Code, our [best Claude Code skills for developers](https://agentskills.codes/blog/best-claude-code-skills-for-developers-2026) post overlaps on Webapp Testing and Code Reviewer, and [AGENTS.md vs CLAUDE.md vs SKILL.md vs Cursor rules](https://agentskills.codes/blog/agents-md-vs-claude-md-vs-skill-md-vs-cursor-rules) explains which file type holds which kind of instruction.

## Best for X: a decision table

| You want to... | Install | Why |
| :-- | :-- | :-- |
| Verify a web app in a real browser | Webapp Testing | Server lifecycle plus a proven pattern |
| Keep tests in JavaScript | Playwright Skill | Node Playwright, MIT, fresher folder |
| Avoid generic-looking UI | Frontend Design | Targets the common generated-design tells |
| Generate a design system from a product type | UI UX Pro Max | Queryable data, most installs |
| Fix a red PR | GH Fix CI | Log script and approval gate |
| Triage review feedback | GH Address Comments | Numbered threads, you choose |
| Review your own diff | Code Reviewer | Local and remote PR in one skill |
| Check UI for accessibility rules | Web Design Guidelines | Live rules, terse output |
| Turn Figma into components | Figma Implement Design | Strict seven-step workflow |
| Review security of a Next.js or React app | Security Best Practices | Framework reference files |
| Keep monorepo config current | Turborepo | Reads docs for the installed version |
| Get App Router habits right | Next.js Best Practices | Compact decision tables |
| Browser automation across agents | Agent Browser | Version-matched CLI guidance |
| Install anything else | Skill Installer | Bundled with Codex |

## Common mistakes

1. **Installing 20 skills at once.** Codex's initial skill list is capped at 2% of the context window, and descriptions are shortened first when it fills up. More skills can mean less precise matching, not more help.
2. **Installing the zip and assuming scripts came along.** The hosted zips we opened were single-file. Skills whose value is a script or a reference folder need a GitHub install.
3. **Putting skills in the wrong folder.** The docs list `.agents/skills`, not `.claude/skills`. A copy in `.claude/skills` is invisible to Codex based on the docs we read.
4. **Trusting install counts.** The most-installed skill here has 1,909 installs; a skill bundled with Codex shows 29. Neither number says how good the instructions are.
5. **Using Claude-only paths and tool names.** `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}`, `WebFetch` and `~/.claude/...` appear in some picks. Edit them or the skill will fail quietly.
6. **Duplicate names.** Codex does not merge two skills with the same `name`; both show up in selectors. Keep one copy per name.
7. **Letting a skill replace your project rules.** Put repository-specific rules in `AGENTS.md` and use skills for repeatable procedures.
8. **Skipping the read.** A SKILL.md is executable instruction. Read it before installing, especially if it runs scripts or fetches URLs.

## Setup checklist

1. Decide scope: repo (`.agents/skills` in the repository), user (`~/.agents/skills`) or admin (`/etc/codex/skills`).
2. Install the one or two skills that match your next task, not the whole list.
3. For script-based skills, use `$skill-installer` or the installer script with `--dest`.
4. Check prerequisites: `gh auth status` for the GitHub skills, a Figma MCP connection for Figma Implement Design, Python or Node for the browser skills.
5. Start Codex in the repository and run `/skills`, or type `$` and look for the name. If it is missing, restart Codex.
6. Try an explicit call first (`$webapp-testing check the signup flow`), then see whether implicit matching picks it up.
7. If a skill fires when you do not want it, disable it with a `[[skills.config]]` entry in `~/.codex/config.toml` rather than deleting it.
8. Re-check upstream before relying on a skill: look at the folder's last commit, not the repository's.

## How we evaluated

**Candidate discovery.** On November 26, 2026 we queried the agentskills.codes API (`/api/v1/skills?q=<term>&limit=50`) for codex, web, nextjs, testing and review, plus follow-up terms such as playwright, frontend, react, security, deploy and accessibility. A `q=codex` query returned 191 skills. We sorted by installs locally, because adding `sort=installs` did not visibly change the order we got. We shortlisted 22 candidates, read each one's catalog page, followed its source link to GitHub, and read 16 SKILL.md files.

**Upstream checks.** For each pick we used the GitHub API to read stars, license, archive status, repository push date, the last commit touching the skill's own folder, and the folder's file listing. We downloaded the catalog's hosted zip for each pick and compared it with the upstream file.

**What we ran.**

- With `CODEX_HOME` pointed at a scratch directory, we ran OpenAI's `install-skill-from-github.py` to install GH Fix CI and Webapp Testing from GitHub. Both installed; a repeat install refused to overwrite.
- We ran `inspect_pr_checks.py --help` from GH Fix CI.
- We ran `list-skills.py`; it failed with an HTTP 403 from GitHub.
- We confirmed `codex-cli 0.166.0` is installed on our research machine. We did **not** launch a Codex session, so we did not watch Codex discover or invoke any of these skills.

**What we did not test.** We did not run Webapp Testing's server script, Agent Browser, Playwright Skill, UI UX Pro Max's search, or the Figma and security workflows. Everything we say about how those behave comes from their SKILL.md text. We did not benchmark output quality; no neutral benchmark exists for individual skills, and we did not invent one. Star counts, installs and prices are vendor or platform-reported and dated.

**Ranking caveat.** Our order weighs usefulness for web development first, so a 7-install skill can outrank a 1,909-install one. That is a judgement, shown in the master table so you can disagree with it.

## FAQ

### Where does Codex load skills from?

From repository, user, admin and system locations. In a repository Codex scans `.agents/skills` in every directory from your working directory up to the repository root. For one user it reads `$HOME/.agents/skills`. Machine-wide skills go in `/etc/codex/skills`, and OpenAI bundles `skill-creator` and `skill-installer`. Source: OpenAI's Codex skills page, read November 26, 2026.

### What is the Codex skills location on my machine?

For personal skills, `~/.agents/skills`. The bundled installer defaults to `~/.codex/skills` (or `$CODEX_HOME/skills`), which our reading of the current `openai/codex` source shows as a real discovery root, but the docs table does not list it. We prefer `~/.agents/skills` because the docs name it.

### Is there a Codex skills directory or library?

Yes, several. OpenAI's own curated set is the [openai/skills](https://github.com/openai/skills) repository (29,160 stars as of November 26, 2026), organized into `.curated`, `.system` and experimental folders, and `$skill-installer` installs from it. The [agentskills.codes catalog](https://agentskills.codes) is a third-party index that lists 11,524 skills with install counts. OpenAI also points to plugins as the distribution channel for reusable skills.

### Is there a Codex skills marketplace?

OpenAI's docs describe a plugin directory shared by ChatGPT and Codex for distributing skills and connectors. We covered skills here, not that directory, and we did not evaluate its listings.

### Are Codex skills the same as Claude Code skills?

The format is the same: a folder with a `SKILL.md` that has `name` and `description`, optionally with scripts, references and assets. OpenAI's docs say skills build on the open agent skills standard. What differs is the folder each tool scans, tool names inside some skills, and Codex-only metadata such as `agents/openai.yaml`.

### Can I use a Claude Code skill in Codex?

Usually, if you copy it to a Codex location and fix any Claude-specific paths or tool names. Read the SKILL.md first. Examples from this list: Webapp Testing and Frontend Design are plain and portable; UI UX Pro Max's catalog copy references a Claude plugin root variable.

### How do I install a Codex skill from GitHub?

Inside Codex, use `$skill-installer` and give it a repo path or URL, or run `install-skill-from-github.py --url <tree-url> --dest <skills-dir>`. We ran the second form on November 26, 2026 and it installed the skill's full folder. Codex should detect it automatically; if not, restart.

### How do I call a skill explicitly?

Type `$` in the CLI or IDE extension and pick the skill, or run `/skills`. You can also just describe the task and let Codex match the skill from its description, which is why a concise, front-loaded description matters.

### How many skills can Codex hold at once?

There is no fixed count in the docs we read. The limit is the context budget for the initial list: 2% of the model's context window by default (8,000 characters if unknown), configurable up to 10,000 tokens with `skills.max_context_tokens`. When it overflows, Codex shortens descriptions first and may omit skills with a warning.

### How do I turn a skill off without deleting it?

Add a `[[skills.config]]` entry to `~/.codex/config.toml` with `path = "/path/to/skill/SKILL.md"` and `enabled = false`, or select by `name`. When several entries match, the last one wins.

### Are these skills safe to install?

They are instructions that can include scripts. We read each SKILL.md and checked folder contents, but we did not security-audit the scripts. Install from sources you trust, read what you install, and prefer repo-scoped installs you can review in version control.

### Do Codex skills cost extra?

No separate fee for the skills in this list. You pay for Codex through your plan or API usage (see the pricing table). Some skills depend on other services, such as Figma's MCP server or cloud browser providers; those have their own terms that we did not verify.

### Which skill should a web developer install first?

Webapp Testing for verification, then Frontend Design for UI work, then GH Fix CI if you live in pull requests. Add the Next.js, Turborepo and security skills when your stack matches.

